<?php

declare(strict_types=1);

namespace Database\Seeders;

use Illuminate\Database\Seeder;
use LauroGuedes\DemoMode\Facades\Demo;

/**
 * The data a visitor lands in.
 *
 * Runs on every reset, against an empty database. Two things are worth deciding
 * before writing it:
 *
 * What a stranger should see. A demo with three rows demonstrates nothing; a demo
 * with a thousand takes long enough to rebuild that visitors notice the
 * maintenance page. Seed the amount that makes the product look like itself.
 *
 * What a stranger should not see. Everything here is public — names, addresses,
 * message bodies, uploaded files. Faker output is fine. A dump of real data with
 * the emails changed is not, and it is the most common way a demo leaks something.
 *
 * The published password comes from Demo::passwordFor(), which the reset stages
 * just before this runs. Reading it here rather than hardcoding one is what keeps
 * the login page and the database in agreement without either side knowing the
 * value.
 *
 * Note the fallback. Outside a reset there is nothing staged, and 'db:seed' on
 * its own is the first thing anybody runs — so the fallback is what stops this
 * seeder failing the first time it is used. Pick something you do not mind
 * appearing in a local database.
 */
class DemoSeeder extends Seeder
{
    public function run(): void
    {
        // $email = config('demo.credentials.accounts.0.email');
        //
        // \App\Models\User::factory()->create([
        //     'email' => $email,
        //     'name' => 'Demo Administrator',
        //     'password' => bcrypt(Demo::passwordFor($email) ?? 'password'),
        // ]);
    }
}
